  "final_text": "All four steps are done. I read the dashboard and the rule back after the last change, and the rule is enabled.\n\n1. **Data view:** \"Kibana Sample Data Logs\" (id `90943e30-9a47-11e8-b64d-95841ca0b247`) points at `kibana_sample_data_logs`, and its time field is `timestamp`.\n   - **Main fields:** `timestamp`, `@timestamp` and `utc_time` (dates); `response` and `response.keyword` (the HTTP response code, stored as text and keyword); `bytes`, `memory`, `machine.ram`, `phpmemory` and `hour_of_day` (numbers); `clientip` and `ip`; `geo.src`, `geo.dest` and `geo.coordinates`; `agent`, `host`, `url`, `request`, `referer`, `extension`, `machine.os`, `tags` and `message` (most with `.keyword` versions).\n   - **Response codes in the data:** only 200 (12,832 requests), 404 (801) and 503 (441).\n2. **Dashboard:** \"S4-679d104f Web logs\" (id `b4b55674-e47c-4591-9b36-c9a8cd56347e`) has exactly two panels, both on that data view:\n   - \"Requests over time\": a line chart counting requests by `timestamp`.\n   - \"Requests by response code\": a bar chart counting requests for each `response.keyword` value.\n3. **Alert rule:** \"S4-679d104f 5xx errors\" (id `5f623f53-6435-494b-818e-004ca639bca8`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It checks every minute and fires when more than 10 matching requests arrive within 5 minutes. It matches every request with a response code from 500 to 599, not just 503. It was created enabled, and its first run succeeded.\n   - The response code is stored as text, not a number, so the 500\u2013599 range compares it as text. That works for normal three-digit codes but would also count an odd value like \"5000\".\n4. **Edit and check:** the response-code panel is now titled \"Responses by code\", and the other panel is unchanged. Reading the rule back shows it enabled, running every minute, with its last run succeeding.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
